00:02:26.919 --> 00:02:35.919
She is the founder and CEO of SciCrunch, in
addition to being a part of the NIF, the Neuroscience

00:02:35.919 --> 00:02:39.219
Information Framework.

00:02:39.219 --> 00:02:45.980
And she works at the Center of Research and
Biological System at UCSD on these two things,

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:47.760
NIF and SciCrunch.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.780
And NIF and SciCrunch work together in order
to aggregate vast amounts of data.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:59.849
Though she's really an expert in this area
of knowing what to do with the data.

00:02:59.849 --> 00:03:07.799
So I really look forward to her talk, and
I know we all really appreciate that you are

00:03:07.799 --> 00:03:13.090
taking the time, Dr. Bandrowski, to come talk
with us today.

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:16.430
And so with that, I think please take away.

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:21.760
The floor is all yours.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:29.459
Thank you so much.

00:03:29.459 --> 00:03:35.959
And what I will do in just a second to switch
over to my slide deck, and I will then mute

00:03:35.959 --> 00:03:37.090
my speakers.

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:47.299
So if you need to get a hold of me, please
do so in the message box.

00:03:47.299 --> 00:03:55.359
So I think now we will have better,
clearer voice.

00:03:55.359 --> 00:04:04.089
So in this guide to the fundamentals of data
science, as Michele had pointed out very poignantly,

00:04:04.089 --> 00:04:07.249
this is an overview of data representation.

00:04:07.249 --> 00:04:11.599
And I have worked on the Neuroscience Information
Framework for about 10 years.

00:04:11.599 --> 00:04:17.660
And the goal of that particular project at
the University of California San Diego is

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:26.340
to make as much data available and useful
to people, mostly to neuroscientists.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:32.700
And so many of the examples that I will use
will actually be from that particular project.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:39.930
But I do also recognize that there are some
core fundamental things that happen.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:43.840
So in terms of the overview of data representation.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:50.510
The lecture series will cover things like
types of data, databases, and data warehouses.

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:56.970
I think it's wonderful we have a wonderful
list of speakers, including a Dr Chaitan Baru,

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:02.700
who also used to be up the road from us at
the supercomputing center.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:10.400
Doctors Picone, and I am going to butcher
Doctor Harabagiu's name, and some of the others.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:15.180
But I wanted to point out when these lectures
are and what are the sorts of things they're

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:16.180
covering.

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:22.680
Today I will cover in a small amount of detail
the basics of databases, the basics of data

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:29.160
wrangling, and then you should be able to
know a whole lot more from Doctor Baru and

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:32.590
others.

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:37.530
So one of the very basic questions is, what
is data?

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:44.200
And I just wanted to make sure that all of
the vocabulary terms have definitions, as

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:48.080
this is supposed to be kind of a primer lecture.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:52.800
So data is the facts and statistics collected
together for reference or analysis.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:57.740
We'll also refer to metadata, which is data
about data.

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:02.180
And actually, when you think about it, one
person's metadata is another person's data,

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:04.500
so then you could have metadata of metadata.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.300
And can go on from there.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:11.061
But another term that I think you should know
is what is a schema?

00:06:11.061 --> 00:06:13.330
And it's basically the structure of the data.

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:14.370
What does it look like?

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:18.770
And this has a very set of specific meanings
when you start to talk about databases.

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:22.710
What do we look across biology?

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:24.070
Data is incredibly diverse.

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:29.440
And again, this is one of the examples from
neuroscience, but you can substitute the brain

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:34.690
into a kidney, and to a liver, and to whatever
else you might want to look at, and essentially

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:38.570
the problem is extraordinarily similar.

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:45.280
Biologists think about interactions of very,
very tiny things.

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:49.490
They look at NMR, they look at other spectroscopies.

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:59.560
They look at data types of that sort on Brownian
motion, and they look all the way to the social

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:03.080
interactions of animal herds.

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:11.550
In fact there is a fun study published by
a group of ecologists-- also biologists--

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:17.050
trying to figure out how to use Google Maps
to determine whether cows point mostly north-south

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:21.110
or east-west when they were going to use themselves
in herds.

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:23.020
And they look at everything in between.

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:26.740
So they were looking at intracellular currents,
they're looking at MRI images.

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:33.230
They were looking at latency to button presses,
and all kinds of other things.

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:39.410
So the data that comes from all of these different
techniques can be incredibly variable.

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:44.680
So really, biological data is characterized
by diversity.

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:48.770
Because it can be text, there can be time
series, it can be categorical, there can be

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:55.620
images, there can be video, there can be locations--
geolocations and locations within the body.

00:07:55.620 --> 00:08:01.389
And all of these things actually are data,
and they're data for different biologists.

00:08:01.389 --> 00:08:05.990
But when we look at data type, it's a little
bit different than that.

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:11.300
So when I was trying to prepare for this lecture,
there was this piece of data on my desktop,

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:13.810
and so here it is.

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:19.330
And there is application, specificity, clone,
quantity, and quantity type.

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:24.010
And when a biologist looks at this particular
piece of data-- this just happened to be on

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:32.070
my desk last week-- they might start thinking
about what this data actually means.

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:37.010
So one of the things that I've learned by
being part of the Neuroscience Information

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:42.860
Framework is that biologists think about data
very differently from computer scientists.

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:46.339
And biologists tend to imbue the data with
meaning.

00:08:46.339 --> 00:08:48.540
So what does it mean to me?

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:50.399
Well, application, WB.

00:08:50.399 --> 00:08:52.670
OK, that's western blot.

00:08:52.670 --> 00:08:55.480
So I need an antibody that works for western
blot.

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:59.610
Clearly this is a piece of data about antibodies.

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:06.589
And these are some of the things here in the
pink boxes that a biologist might think about

00:09:06.589 --> 00:09:09.110
when they see this piece of data.

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:15.279
But if you're a computer scientist, or a data
scientist, you might start thinking about

00:09:15.279 --> 00:09:19.939
this same piece of data, the same exact spreadsheet,
slightly differently.

00:09:19.939 --> 00:09:24.279
They might need to think about what the structure
of this data is.

00:09:24.279 --> 00:09:27.129
That there is an IF comma WB.

00:09:27.129 --> 00:09:29.980
This is a comma delimited list.

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:35.379
They might think about the specificity not
as something that I might or might not use,

00:09:35.379 --> 00:09:39.449
but they're actually thinking about, well,
what is the external lookup that will be required

00:09:39.449 --> 00:09:42.309
in order to explain this specificity better?

00:09:42.309 --> 00:09:45.680
This is actually a lookup to NCBI gene.

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:49.329
Clone number they're a little bit confused
about, because this is free text.

00:09:49.329 --> 00:09:53.899
But quantities and quantity types, they're
thinking about the types of operations that

00:09:53.899 --> 00:10:00.379
they might do on data that is numerical.

00:10:00.379 --> 00:10:07.870
So data type is a particular kind of data
item defined by the values it can take-- whether

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:15.730
it's text or number-- the programming language
that's used, or the operations that can be

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:16.930
performed on it.

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:21.869
So again, we can do things to numbers that
we cannot do to a list.

00:10:21.869 --> 00:10:25.120
And so if we look across disciplines, it's
not the same thing.

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:30.860
So when computer scientists look at data,
they imbue it with structure.

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:33.819
And data types, I've listed some here, there
are many more.

00:10:33.819 --> 00:10:36.199
These come from SQL.

00:10:36.199 --> 00:10:43.220
Data types are obviously text controlled vocabularies,
which you heard about from Doctor Musin and

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:46.999
actually Doctor [INAUDIBLE] and others.

00:10:46.999 --> 00:10:51.901
There are gene names, which these are the
sorts of things there are.

00:10:51.901 --> 00:10:58.649
They're free text, there are all kinds of
different types of numbers, and there are,

00:10:58.649 --> 00:11:01.290
of course, dates and other things.

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:07.230
But why do we care about data types?

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:11.320
Turns out the only time you really deeply
care about data types is when

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:14.050
you want to put data into a database.

00:11:14.050 --> 00:11:20.199
And a database is essentially a structured
set of data that's held in a computer, especially

00:11:20.199 --> 00:11:23.190
one that's accessible in various ways.

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:30.569
So really to understand what a database is
I wanted to go back and just ask, what are

00:11:30.569 --> 00:11:33.230
some of the things that a database can do?

00:11:33.230 --> 00:11:37.480
It's essentially a container for different
types of data.

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:39.120
It's a container for data files.

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:42.769
You can think of it as a file system on your
computer.

00:11:42.769 --> 00:11:50.559
So our little example here for this spreadsheet
of antibodies could be part of the database

00:11:50.559 --> 00:11:53.970
as long as it's accessible on a computer.

00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:59.089
So what are the kinds of operations that I
could do if this was part of a database?

00:11:59.089 --> 00:12:07.089
Well, I could do things like count the number
of rows that have western blot, that are like

00:12:07.089 --> 00:12:08.389
western blot.

00:12:08.389 --> 00:12:10.320
Which antibodies work for western blot?

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:11.350
You can get that.

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:15.029
And the answer would be all four of these.

00:12:15.029 --> 00:12:20.639
You can select rows where specificity actually
is one of these zinc figure proteins, or the

00:12:20.639 --> 00:12:21.759
other one.

00:12:21.759 --> 00:12:27.420
And then it would bring you back the data
about that particular row or that particular

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:30.670
data item, depending on what you asked.

00:12:30.670 --> 00:12:36.290
You could find all rows where quantity is
larger than or smaller than a particular value.

00:12:36.290 --> 00:12:44.620
So essentially the database is a container
that's very useful in many ways in order to

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:48.440
ask questions about a particular dataset.

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:54.660
But you can do this as a biologist, and I
bring this piece in to this overview lecture

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:57.970
because most biologists use Excel.

00:12:57.970 --> 00:13:01.580
And Excel is an incredibly powerful tool.

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:05.449
And most people know all kinds of things about
Excel.

00:13:05.449 --> 00:13:10.639
It is one of these programs that's incredibly
powerful, incredibly ubiquitous.

00:13:10.639 --> 00:13:14.499
But also every tool has its limitations.

00:13:14.499 --> 00:13:19.269
So I wanted to make sure that whoever doesn't
know about these limitations knows about them

00:13:19.269 --> 00:13:21.529
after this lecture.

00:13:21.529 --> 00:13:27.819
So one of the limitations of Excel is size.

00:13:27.819 --> 00:13:34.410
So there is a certain number of data points,
a certain number of data rows and data columns

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:37.059
that Excel will take.

00:13:37.059 --> 00:13:42.189
And it is important to understand if we have
data of a certain size, a certain number of

00:13:42.189 --> 00:13:48.439
rows, that simply would be too large for Excel
to take.

00:13:48.439 --> 00:13:56.939
And so it's one of the limitations that everyone
needs to be concerned about and aware of of

00:13:56.939 --> 00:13:57.939
Excel.

00:13:57.939 --> 00:14:02.929
The most recent-- and actually for the last
probably five, 10 years-- versions of Excel

00:14:02.929 --> 00:14:07.430
have been getting more and more aggressive
in terms of autocorrecting.

00:14:07.430 --> 00:14:13.899
So autocorrecting here in this particular
article-- this is now quite old, but it hasn't

00:14:13.899 --> 00:14:23.380
changed so we should continue to be aware
of it-- it is that gene names actually become

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:24.429
dates.

00:14:24.429 --> 00:14:30.040
So this is something that my Excel did, and
I have the latest version of Excel running

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:32.149
on a Mac.

00:14:32.149 --> 00:14:36.769
And so what this is doing is, I put in the
APR 4 gene.

00:14:36.769 --> 00:14:42.129
As soon as I hit enter, it became the 4th
of April.

00:14:42.129 --> 00:14:46.850
And the way that that's coded in Excel is
the number 42 464.

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:47.899
64.

00:14:47.899 --> 00:14:56.300
So if I put in 1,000 genes into my particular
spreadsheet, and some of them were APR 4 genes,

00:14:56.300 --> 00:14:59.860
then I would have a very large mistake in
my spreadsheet.

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:05.629
And this has certainly been called out in
this paper and many others.

00:15:05.629 --> 00:15:08.819
So it's something that one must be aware of.

00:15:08.819 --> 00:15:16.970
The other problem with this is that when you
change from an Excel sheet that's actually

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:25.629
in a Mac to one that's in a PC, and so you're
passing a file over to your graduate student,

00:15:25.629 --> 00:15:32.799
your PI, your fellow at whatever institute,
and they happen to have a different operating

00:15:32.799 --> 00:15:35.360
system and a different version of Excel.

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:42.190
What ends up happening is that mistakes like
this, which now become coded-- so April 4th

00:15:42.190 --> 00:15:49.649
becomes coded as this particular number--
that number is only April 4th for a PC, or

00:15:49.649 --> 00:15:52.929
it's only April 4th for Mac.

00:15:52.929 --> 00:15:56.230
It will not be the same number if you switch
operating systems.

00:15:56.230 --> 00:16:06.709
So in a sense it is a really bad idea to handle
a lot of data, especially large data where

00:16:06.709 --> 00:16:13.829
there are genes involved on Excel it is an
extremely bad idea because there are these

00:16:13.829 --> 00:16:21.449
known differences between the dates the date
columns and how Excel actually handles them.

00:16:21.449 --> 00:16:25.429
This is another type of mistake that is very
common.

00:16:25.429 --> 00:16:31.929
So here I've taken the ZNF 622 gene, and instead
of copying and pasting that gene I just drag

00:16:31.929 --> 00:16:36.889
down the little icon.

00:16:36.889 --> 00:16:44.069
And the newer versions of Excel, again, will
not only iterate by one number when you only

00:16:44.069 --> 00:16:51.499
have a number, but they will actually iterate
to add one number to almost any column that

00:16:51.499 --> 00:16:53.489
has a number in it.

00:16:53.489 --> 00:16:58.249
So this is another thing that one must be
aware of, and I see a lot of data, and this

00:16:58.249 --> 00:17:06.130
is the kind of mistake that I see unfortunately
quite often.

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:09.280
So how can I really understand a database?

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:17.271
So again, in order to get biologists to understand
what computer scientists are thinking, I want

00:17:17.271 --> 00:17:22.160
to bring in some of the things that computer
scientists and data scientists would be actually

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:25.940
concerned with when they see our file.

00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:31.750
So computer scientists very, very, very, very
concerned with efficiency of data retrieval,

00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:37.750
with the size of the total file, with the
data types, which we just discussed, and with

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:41.460
the types of operations that need to be done
on that data.

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:49.530
So this particular Excel file, actually I
have saved it on my desktop in different formats.

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:54.950
And this is something that we've all done,
and I'm sure that all biologists basically

00:17:54.950 --> 00:18:00.030
largely ignore this unless there's some reason
to pay attention to it.

00:18:00.030 --> 00:18:07.960
So this particular file is a 46 kilobyte Excel
file save as latest version of Excel.

00:18:07.960 --> 00:18:14.261
If I save the same information as a PDF it
actually becomes a 12 kilobyte file, so you

00:18:14.261 --> 00:18:16.870
can see that there's a very large difference.

00:18:16.870 --> 00:18:21.530
But as CSV or text it actually becomes much,
much smaller.

00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:27.650
So that really reflects something that, again,
for most biologists, we don't care.

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:31.120
My computer is large enough to handle any
of these kind of files.

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:36.800
But the larger the file size you have, actually
the more you have to care about this sort

00:18:36.800 --> 00:18:41.460
of question, these sort of ideas.

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:52.390
So again, understanding a database is really
taking this particular example of this file.

00:18:52.390 --> 00:19:00.080
What we can see is that computer scientists,
because they are very concerned with the process

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:06.250
of efficiency of data storage, efficiency
of data retrieval, and the size of data, they

00:19:06.250 --> 00:19:17.760
have driven these spreadsheets down from 153
bytes to even smaller and smaller sizes so

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:20.980
that you can actually store much more data
in the same space.

00:19:20.980 --> 00:19:28.330
And one of the ways that this happens is through
a process called normalization of data.

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:36.340
So here I've normalized this particular table,
the one in yellow, into the two tables in

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:37.340
green.

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:40.720
And the column I've normalized is the specificity
column.

00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:45.180
So here you will notice that there's a zinc
finger protein here, there's a different one

00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:52.480
here, and there are two rows for for zip kinase.

00:19:52.480 --> 00:20:00.570
And now the way that we can make this spreadsheet
even smaller is actually to code the fact

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:08.920
that zinc finger protein 622 is actually now
corresponding to the number one, the other

00:20:08.920 --> 00:20:14.770
zinc finger is two, and these two zip kinases
are both 3.

00:20:14.770 --> 00:20:19.370
And now there is something called a key value
pair.

00:20:19.370 --> 00:20:24.290
So the number one corresponds to this, number
two this, number three, this.

00:20:24.290 --> 00:20:30.800
And what that does over millions of rows is
it further decreases the size of the data,

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:32.000
which is great.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:36.360
It speeds up retrieval of data, which is great.

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:42.070
But the more rows and the more columns that
actually look like this, the more data that

00:20:42.070 --> 00:20:46.240
looks like this, the less readable it is by
a human.

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:50.940
This spreadsheet, I would argue, is relatively
well readable by a human.

00:20:50.940 --> 00:21:01.040
But once you start changing out a lot of the
actual labels with numbers, what starts to

00:21:01.040 --> 00:21:03.450
happen is that you can no longer read it.

00:21:03.450 --> 00:21:12.960
You have to do a lookup for each thing, and
those kind of operations to make the spreadsheet

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:19.030
back into something that's human readable
really starts to take a data wrangler.

00:21:19.030 --> 00:21:23.020
And I'll talk a little bit more about this.

00:21:23.020 --> 00:21:27.410
So what I'm talking about here are a lot of
relational databases.

00:21:27.410 --> 00:21:32.910
Now relational databases, the reason that
I'm talking about them is because they handle

00:21:32.910 --> 00:21:35.350
very large files very, very well.

00:21:35.350 --> 00:21:39.840
So you don't have to stick to just a million
rows of data.

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:44.010
These are some of the oldest and the most
common databases throughout the internet.

00:21:44.010 --> 00:21:47.930
They run banks, they run stores, they run
all kinds of transactions, they run many,

00:21:47.930 --> 00:21:48.930
many websites.

00:21:48.930 --> 00:21:53.780
Everywhere you have a box you probably are
filling out a form that goes into a database.

00:21:53.780 --> 00:21:59.290
So we have a lot of interaction with databases,
even if we don't necessarily know that we

00:21:59.290 --> 00:22:00.290
do.

00:22:00.290 --> 00:22:06.310
They do allow multiple users, including multiple
machines, to actually add data simultaneously

00:22:06.310 --> 00:22:08.990
to the same data store.

00:22:08.990 --> 00:22:13.230
They allow data to be extracted at the same
time as it's put in.

00:22:13.230 --> 00:22:16.660
There are lots and lots of things that people
can do if with these.

00:22:16.660 --> 00:22:22.210
There's a tremendous amount of diversity in
terms of what can they be programmed to actually

00:22:22.210 --> 00:22:27.660
do, and there is, of course, a programming
interface which makes everything very flexible.

00:22:27.660 --> 00:22:34.520
But as every tool, it is very, very understandable
that there are some limitations.

00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:39.650
So one of the first limitations for biologists
specifically is that these kinds of databases

00:22:39.650 --> 00:22:45.190
are actually more difficult to set up than
an Excel file, or a Google Doc.

00:22:45.190 --> 00:22:51.260
It is more difficult to do some of the simple
operations like copy this data into that particular

00:22:51.260 --> 00:22:52.260
space.

00:22:52.260 --> 00:22:56.250
There is no undo button, there is no autocorrect.

00:22:56.250 --> 00:23:01.530
Which is either a limitation or a high benefit,
depending on where you sit.

00:23:01.530 --> 00:23:05.110
And they are very dependent on data structures.

00:23:05.110 --> 00:23:10.790
So you have to predefine what you want to
actually have in the database before you can

00:23:10.790 --> 00:23:11.950
actually fill in the data.

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:12.950
in the database before you can actually fill
in the data.

00:23:12.950 --> 00:23:20.030
So this is something that is a limitation
that many computer scientists have actually

00:23:20.030 --> 00:23:23.930
considered as a very significant limitation.

00:23:23.930 --> 00:23:32.590
And they have created a whole bunch of new
types of databases, which essentially are

00:23:32.590 --> 00:23:34.910
much newer, less common.

00:23:34.910 --> 00:23:43.540
And most of these database types have been
created to solve one or more of the shortcomings

00:23:43.540 --> 00:23:46.600
of the standard traditional relational databases.

00:23:46.600 --> 00:23:53.320
So Neo4J, for example, does a lot of very
efficient computation on graph structures,

00:23:53.320 --> 00:23:57.130
and I think that will be covered by Doctor
Baru and others.

00:23:57.130 --> 00:24:02.170
MongoDB does really wonderful things with
text storage and retrieval being very, very

00:24:02.170 --> 00:24:07.780
much more efficient than the conventional
SQL databases.

00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:15.501
But new tools, as all new tools, have smaller
numbers of people that can support them, and

00:24:15.501 --> 00:24:19.930
not all the kinks are usually worked out from
a lot of these newer databases.

00:24:19.930 --> 00:24:26.410
So when you're choosing how to store data
it's important to consider some of these things.

00:24:26.410 --> 00:24:33.710
Not just always go for the newest best thing,
because there may be some lacks of support.

00:24:33.710 --> 00:24:41.220
So really I wanted to ask the question, what
makes a database useful to biologists?

00:24:41.220 --> 00:24:46.980
And this is something I've run into, and my
colleagues have run into.

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:54.630
Because I am trained as a biologist, I see
data from this perspective quite a bit.

00:24:54.630 --> 00:25:01.270
So what computer scientists think that biologists
want to do with databases is actually quite

00:25:01.270 --> 00:25:05.780
different than what biologists actually want
to do with databases.

00:25:05.780 --> 00:25:10.940
And so one of the things that computer scientists
often think is that biologists are just dying

00:25:10.940 --> 00:25:19.520
to create hypotheses based on-- and here I
have pointed out, this is a gratuitous graphic.

00:25:19.520 --> 00:25:25.500
I figured it wouldn't be a big data talk without
one of these lovely hairballs.

00:25:25.500 --> 00:25:30.620
This is lovingly referred to as a data hairball.

00:25:30.620 --> 00:25:38.140
Now, computer scientists really think that
biologists would love to create hypotheses

00:25:38.140 --> 00:25:39.140
based on data.

00:25:39.140 --> 00:25:42.930
Now, I haven't run into many biologists that
actually would like to do this.

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:47.390
Mostly they have a hypothesis in their head
already that's based on reading literature

00:25:47.390 --> 00:25:50.980
and understanding biology.

00:25:50.980 --> 00:25:55.600
But it would be really nice if we could do
that in the future.

00:25:55.600 --> 00:26:02.110
Also, comparing their data to other people's
data, biologists have a really hard time doing

00:26:02.110 --> 00:26:03.110
that.

00:26:03.110 --> 00:26:08.840
And actually it's one of the things that biologists
are really reluctant to be able to do.

00:26:08.840 --> 00:26:14.840
And exploring data space is one of those things
where it's nice, but it often doesn't go right

00:26:14.840 --> 00:26:20.520
into the middle of the biologist's pipeline
in terms of what they're doing in the lab

00:26:20.520 --> 00:26:21.520
actively.

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:27.100
What biologists actually use databases for
is they look up papers.

00:26:27.100 --> 00:26:34.500
Now, I haven't been able to find exact numbers
in terms of the internet traffic to biological

00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:41.960
databases, but I do know from various sources
that the vast majority-and this is a very,

00:26:41.960 --> 00:26:47.020
very high majority-- of all web traffic to
any database in biomedicine actually goes

00:26:47.020 --> 00:26:48.820
just to one.

00:26:48.820 --> 00:26:50.900
That one is PubMed.

00:26:50.900 --> 00:26:55.740
That is a database, and it is where everyone
goes.

00:26:55.740 --> 00:27:00.650
Biologists do share data, and they store data
in databases, but they often don't look for

00:27:00.650 --> 00:27:01.680
data there.

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:06.030
Bio informaticians do, and so that's a whole
different story.

00:27:06.030 --> 00:27:10.510
But biologists tend to just take their data
and put it somewhere.

00:27:10.510 --> 00:27:13.850
And they need to make sure that that's relatively
easy.

00:27:13.850 --> 00:27:18.050
Biologists also are starting to use databases
as a lookup.

00:27:18.050 --> 00:27:23.060
So for example, NCBI gene, I need to store
my data, I need to figure out what other gene

00:27:23.060 --> 00:27:25.400
is related to my gene.

00:27:25.400 --> 00:27:28.420
Is my snip in this place or in that place?

00:27:28.420 --> 00:27:34.800
What are the places, and who else has published
on that particular snip?

00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:37.930
So there's a lookup function.

00:27:37.930 --> 00:27:47.890
But for the lookup function, you need to understand
the database has to be relatively complete.

00:27:47.890 --> 00:27:54.980
So for biologists to actually use a particular
database as a lookup function, it has to have

00:27:54.980 --> 00:27:59.410
that completeness.

00:27:59.410 --> 00:28:03.980
Unless it's useful as a storage device, in
which case there's no question there, and

00:28:03.980 --> 00:28:07.230
completeness is not necessary.

00:28:07.230 --> 00:28:10.520
So how many databases are there currently
for biologists?

00:28:10.520 --> 00:28:16.950
I've tried to look at some of these numbers,
and I try to do some lookups in both WorldCat,

00:28:16.950 --> 00:28:24.360
so this is the library catalog of resources,
including a whole section of internet resources.

00:28:24.360 --> 00:28:29.560
And what they have is about 5,200 databases,
but this covers all fields.

00:28:29.560 --> 00:28:34.350
So it's geology and everything else.

00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:42.220
In the Neuroscience Information Framework,
we have cataloged 2,600 of them essentially.

00:28:42.220 --> 00:28:49.470
So these are all biologically relevant databases,
or largely biologically relevant databases.

00:28:49.470 --> 00:28:51.950
So that's the kind of thing that we're looking
at.

00:28:51.950 --> 00:28:57.460
And NCBI, which is the National Library of
Medicine, there are 150 databases that are

00:28:57.460 --> 00:28:59.700
housed actually inside of NCBI.

00:28:59.700 --> 00:29:03.610
Of course, everyone knows PubMed.

00:29:03.610 --> 00:29:09.080
Most people know PubMed Central, but there
is actually 150 others, including some that

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:12.650
you probably know, and many that you've never
heard of.

00:29:12.650 --> 00:29:20.440
But I would encourage all people to explore
where some of these other databases are At

00:29:20.440 --> 00:29:25.950
EBI, 53 databases are actually housed, including
Europe PubMed Central.

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:30.620
So a way to get articles for Europeans.

00:29:30.620 --> 00:29:36.850
ArrayExpress, which is very similar to Geo,
ChEMBL, and others.

00:29:36.850 --> 00:29:43.820
But the biologists might ask, with all of
these different databases-- and here I've

00:29:43.820 --> 00:29:50.410
actually pulled out some of the keywords from
the neuroscience information framework system

00:29:50.410 --> 00:29:56.350
for all databases, and the organisms that
have been tacked to those particular database

00:29:56.350 --> 00:29:57.570
entries.

00:29:57.570 --> 00:30:04.520
So essentially, if you're a biologist and
you're looking for a human genetics database,

00:30:04.520 --> 00:30:10.760
you will have hundreds to choose from.

00:30:10.760 --> 00:30:16.070
So if you have the choice of trying to find
your data in hundreds of databases, or thousands

00:30:16.070 --> 00:30:19.830
of databases, you might ask some questions
as a biologist.

00:30:19.830 --> 00:30:21.270
How many of these are still alive?

00:30:21.270 --> 00:30:23.590
How many of these are still being added to?

00:30:23.590 --> 00:30:26.650
Which ones are being cited, which ones are
being used?

00:30:26.650 --> 00:30:29.100
Which ones house actually the same data?

00:30:29.100 --> 00:30:36.270
So we know that a ArrayExpress and Geo actually
house many of the same data sets.

00:30:36.270 --> 00:30:38.110
Which ones are the gold standard?

00:30:38.110 --> 00:30:41.820
Where do I put my data so that it gets saved
and kept?

00:30:41.820 --> 00:30:46.510
And this particular question is not just being
asked by biologists, but it's also being asked

00:30:46.510 --> 00:30:47.950
by the journals.

00:30:47.950 --> 00:30:54.120
Because the journals need to ask the question,
OK, I have an author who has a particular

00:30:54.120 --> 00:30:55.560
type of data.

00:30:55.560 --> 00:30:58.390
Where do I send that author to deposit that
data?

00:30:58.390 --> 00:31:00.380
We know that depositing the data is important.

00:31:00.380 --> 00:31:05.640
We want to go along with the standards that
are being put forward by the granting agencies,

00:31:05.640 --> 00:31:07.080
but where do I go?

00:31:07.080 --> 00:31:11.540
There are 2,600 databases that might be relevant
to them.

00:31:11.540 --> 00:31:15.520
Which ones are the ones I should actually
point them to?

00:31:15.520 --> 00:31:20.220
Computer scientists actually look at this
landscape and think other thoughts.

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:24.050
They think, well, which ones are relational,
which ones are MongoDB based?

00:31:24.050 --> 00:31:27.770
Which ones have this or that kind of schema?

00:31:27.770 --> 00:31:31.150
How can I get data out of this or that one?

00:31:31.150 --> 00:31:36.490
They're looking also at standards, and which
ones are structured in a particular way.

00:31:36.490 --> 00:31:42.680
And again, Susanne [INAUDIBLE] I would encourage
you, if you hadn't seen the last lecture to

00:31:42.680 --> 00:31:45.010
see the last lecture in this series.

00:31:45.010 --> 00:31:52.160
But she did a very nice job discussing some
of this in terms of the standards of databases

00:31:52.160 --> 00:31:57.070
that are being used through bio sharing.

00:31:57.070 --> 00:32:02.440
So if I'm a biologist, there are 30 databases
relevant to me.

00:32:02.440 --> 00:32:06.570
How do I navigate, inquiry this number?

00:32:06.570 --> 00:32:10.760
Because we have to remember, databases are
not completely standard.

00:32:10.760 --> 00:32:15.400
In fact, they're very likely to not be very
standard.

00:32:15.400 --> 00:32:17.260
Each one has its own interface.

00:32:17.260 --> 00:32:20.530
Each one has its own organizational system.

00:32:20.530 --> 00:32:22.520
Each database has its own caveats.

00:32:22.520 --> 00:32:30.000
Each database may reference a completely different
standard in terms of how it stores its data.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:35.350
So how do I navigate this mess, essentially?

00:32:35.350 --> 00:32:39.790
And this is where we actually have to learn
about the data warehouse.

00:32:39.790 --> 00:32:46.330
And the data warehouse is essentially a whole
bunch of databases put into one system so

00:32:46.330 --> 00:32:51.660
that somebody can run a query across those
30 databases.

00:32:51.660 --> 00:32:58.550
But of course, in a data warehouse, what we
can actually start to do is we can ask questions

00:32:58.550 --> 00:33:00.520
of all these multiple databases.

00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:07.210
We can ask a question such as, which Parkinson's
disease causing snip, this [INAUDIBLE] house,

00:33:07.210 --> 00:33:11.110
that may have a homologous mutation in a worm?

00:33:11.110 --> 00:33:15.480
This is something that can be asked of three,
or four, or five databases together, but not

00:33:15.480 --> 00:33:17.270
of any one.

00:33:17.270 --> 00:33:22.300
Because one database may not have all of the
right links, all of the right connections,

00:33:22.300 --> 00:33:24.690
to ask that sort of question.

00:33:24.690 --> 00:33:30.390
So a data warehouse starts to broaden the
scope of a particular database and starts

00:33:30.390 --> 00:33:39.330
to connect the different databases based on
different rules in terms of how a particular

00:33:39.330 --> 00:33:44.350
set of data can interact with another set
of data.

00:33:44.350 --> 00:33:51.140
How can a particular database talk to another
database, and what are the sorts of things.

00:33:51.140 --> 00:34:00.180
So here I'm showing you an example of one
data warehouse system.

00:34:00.180 --> 00:34:06.170
This data warehouse system actually, the thing
I have highlighted here is the RID strain

00:34:06.170 --> 00:34:08.240
data for worms.

00:34:08.240 --> 00:34:11.620
This is a worm base table, which talks about
the

00:34:11.620 --> 00:34:17.640
genotype of these particular c elegans.

00:34:17.640 --> 00:34:24.200
And within this particular database, which
is a data warehouse, there's a lot of other

00:34:24.200 --> 00:34:27.369
data, including neuron DB.

00:34:27.369 --> 00:34:29.990
There's data from worm mine.

00:34:29.990 --> 00:34:34.780
There is data from homology, and there is
data from all of these other databases.

00:34:34.780 --> 00:34:44.260
But then as soon as you start to hold this
amount of data and this kind of data, the

00:34:44.260 --> 00:34:50.950
thing that happens is you're very, very concerned
about when that data actually came in, because

00:34:50.950 --> 00:34:53.200
it might be old.

00:34:53.200 --> 00:34:55.380
What were the data transformations?

00:34:55.380 --> 00:34:58.460
So there is something on worm base.

00:34:58.460 --> 00:35:04.210
Now, we made some assumptions in order to
create these particular tables.

00:35:04.210 --> 00:35:07.190
Those assumptions need to be absolutely explicit.

00:35:07.190 --> 00:35:13.930
Those are data transformations that happen
between what worm base says and what we say

00:35:13.930 --> 00:35:17.290
worm base says.

00:35:17.290 --> 00:35:19.620
So there are these assumptions that are made.

00:35:19.620 --> 00:35:25.020
There is a provenance in terms of when the
data entered our system when the data was

00:35:25.020 --> 00:35:30.720
released from the worm base site is another
set of provenance, right?

00:35:30.720 --> 00:35:35.980
So there are a lot of things to know when
these are metadata about the particular database

00:35:35.980 --> 00:35:37.280
table that you're looking at.

00:35:37.280 --> 00:35:39.140
What am I looking at?

00:35:39.140 --> 00:35:41.180
Very important to know.

00:35:41.180 --> 00:35:47.630
So these are all very meaningful things that
need to be answered.

00:35:47.630 --> 00:35:54.250
So when you do this though, let's say we want
to put together 30 databases.

00:35:54.250 --> 00:36:00.369
Each database basis makes a lot of different
assumptions about the data.

00:36:00.369 --> 00:36:06.500
If I want to do this myself in my lab, will
my programmer computer science colleagues

00:36:06.500 --> 00:36:13.470
actually deal with the kinds of complexity
that a lot of these biological databases house

00:36:13.470 --> 00:36:15.540
and contain?

00:36:15.540 --> 00:36:22.260
Will I be able to interact with them well
enough to get them to understand what it is

00:36:22.260 --> 00:36:29.260
that the data actually is supposed to mean
so that the structure can reflect that properly?

00:36:29.260 --> 00:36:36.480
So data wrangling is a term that's been thrown
around quite a bit.

00:36:36.480 --> 00:36:42.369
But we're starting to have an understanding
of what a data wrangler is.

00:36:42.369 --> 00:36:48.950
But in terms of what is it, it's a process
of extracting data from both tools, databases,

00:36:48.950 --> 00:36:53.860
and basically putting it into your tool or
database or warehouse.

00:36:53.860 --> 00:36:58.110
So it's a data stewardship function.

00:36:58.110 --> 00:37:01.869
But if we want to look at the job description,
what must this person really know?

00:37:01.869 --> 00:37:05.680
If you have a data wrangler, what must they
know?

00:37:05.680 --> 00:37:09.000
Well, this is a fun job description.

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:15.270
But this person must have a deep, deep knowledge
of the meaning of the data.

00:37:15.270 --> 00:37:19.070
So a Ph.D. In biological science really helps.

00:37:19.070 --> 00:37:24.240
Because that person would be expected to understand
the caveats of that data.

00:37:24.240 --> 00:37:27.570
Deep knowledge of clinical and genetic databases.

00:37:27.570 --> 00:37:30.040
Each of these databases, again, has its own
caveats.

00:37:30.040 --> 00:37:33.990
They all take a certain kind of data, they
don't take other data.

00:37:33.990 --> 00:37:39.640
They can be queried in ways that others cannot
be queried.

00:37:39.640 --> 00:37:43.080
Data structures are also very important to
understand for this person.

00:37:43.080 --> 00:37:48.330
So computer languages, statistics, Excel and
Arc all really, really important, as well

00:37:48.330 --> 00:37:52.030
as a knowledge of ontologies, the deeper the
better.

00:37:52.030 --> 00:37:58.720
So the kind of person that needs to do this
wrangling is the person that sits between

00:37:58.720 --> 00:38:01.420
computer science and biology.

00:38:01.420 --> 00:38:09.370
That person must have a deep understanding
of all of the ways that biology has of dealing

00:38:09.370 --> 00:38:13.190
with the data, and all of
the little things that can go wrong with that

00:38:13.190 --> 00:38:15.660
particular data.

00:38:15.660 --> 00:38:18.550
And of course, the computer science, which
is the structure.

00:38:18.550 --> 00:38:25.950
How do you now pull this so that it's properly
structured into a warehouse?

00:38:25.950 --> 00:38:32.880
So the kind of things that biologists do,
which end up having to deal with a whole lot

00:38:32.880 --> 00:38:39.790
of wrangling of data-- and there's a lot of
data wranglers over at Craig Venter's company,

00:38:39.790 --> 00:38:44.870
Human Longevity, which just produced this
very nice paper in proceedings of National

00:38:44.870 --> 00:38:50.060
Academy, where they've just released the deep
sequencing of 10,000 human genomes.

00:38:50.060 --> 00:38:57.110
You can imagine, this is 10,000 data points
for each particular genome, and each genome

00:38:57.110 --> 00:39:04.130
will have many, many, many, many, many, many,
many millions of data points in and of itself.

00:39:04.130 --> 00:39:11.850
So making sure that getting that right, putting
everything into one single warehouse, is actually

00:39:11.850 --> 00:39:18.510
the work of a large number of data wranglers.

00:39:18.510 --> 00:39:24.840
So let's take this down into a more understandable
level.

00:39:24.840 --> 00:39:33.210
So if we think of data wrangling as doing
laundry, there are these terms called manual

00:39:33.210 --> 00:39:34.210
curation.

00:39:34.210 --> 00:39:42.190
So the way that we can think about data wrangling,
if we're thinking laundry terms, is manual

00:39:42.190 --> 00:39:47.060
curation is sorting everything into appropriate
piles.

00:39:47.060 --> 00:39:50.190
There's something called semantic mapping.

00:39:50.190 --> 00:39:55.990
And semantic mapping is assigning the proper
word to each of the piles.

00:39:55.990 --> 00:40:00.010
So here we have shirts in one pile and socks
in another pile.

00:40:00.010 --> 00:40:01.290
So these are two terms.

00:40:01.290 --> 00:40:04.390
Now these piles are semantically mapped.

00:40:04.390 --> 00:40:08.750
There's automatic indexing, and I think you
heard a lot about that previously.

00:40:08.750 --> 00:40:13.310
Now we know that there are 10 shirts and five
socks.

00:40:13.310 --> 00:40:14.410
Wonderful.

00:40:14.410 --> 00:40:15.950
And there's a resource identifier.

00:40:15.950 --> 00:40:19.380
So the research identifier is something I
also want to spend a little bit of time on

00:40:19.380 --> 00:40:24.520
because that's how we can trace back to what
we actually have in the data warehouse.

00:40:24.520 --> 00:40:31.060
So if we look at semantic mapping first, semantic
mapping is essentially adding a term to a

00:40:31.060 --> 00:40:32.910
particular data item.

00:40:32.910 --> 00:40:35.190
And there are different ways to do that.

00:40:35.190 --> 00:40:39.880
Again, Mark Musin and Suzanne [INAUDIBLE]
had actually done a lot of the work in terms

00:40:39.880 --> 00:40:49.070
of explaining how some of these terminologies
get put onto different data systems, so I'm

00:40:49.070 --> 00:40:51.859
not going to talk about it a whole lot here.

00:40:51.859 --> 00:41:03.430
But an ontology is essentially a very, very
structured glossary or thesaurus.

00:41:03.430 --> 00:41:10.400
It's essentially a way of saying this is the
thing that I'm talking about.

00:41:10.400 --> 00:41:15.850
And the semantic clarity of an ontology is
very, very high compared to even a taxonomy.

00:41:15.850 --> 00:41:18.420
A taxonomy is something that we understand.

00:41:18.420 --> 00:41:21.600
We know that mammals are related to rats.

00:41:21.600 --> 00:41:26.310
And we know exactly how they're related because
there are different steps in between those

00:41:26.310 --> 00:41:29.370
two terms.

00:41:29.370 --> 00:41:31.260
But an ontology is even better than that.

00:41:31.260 --> 00:41:38.480
So for example, here when we look at an intelligent
of socks, we know that a sock is a type of

00:41:38.480 --> 00:41:43.230
clothing and clothing to it typically has
part thread.

00:41:43.230 --> 00:41:46.720
So these are all terms that can be related
to one another.

00:41:46.720 --> 00:41:55.510
Now if you say I am going to label my particular
set of data with the word sock, now if I do

00:41:55.510 --> 00:42:01.570
it with just a controlled vocabulary, that's
great because then you can find all socks.

00:42:01.570 --> 00:42:07.901
But if your database has thousands of different
items or millions of different items, maybe

00:42:07.901 --> 00:42:10.960
you want to look for all things that are clothing.

00:42:10.960 --> 00:42:16.250
And if you want to look for all things that
are clothing, if you mark your data with a

00:42:16.250 --> 00:42:23.240
controlled vocabulary, that controlled vocabulary
doesn't know that sock and shirt is related.

00:42:23.240 --> 00:42:28.119
That controlled vocabulary doesn't know that
sock and shirt is a type of clothing.

00:42:28.119 --> 00:42:34.680
But if you mark with an ontology, those relationships
are inside of the system of tagging.

00:42:34.680 --> 00:42:42.070
Which means that what you can actually do
is you can ask a database or a database system

00:42:42.070 --> 00:42:43.690
for all clothing.

00:42:43.690 --> 00:42:45.840
And I'll show you some of that in a little
bit.

00:42:45.840 --> 00:42:50.240
Well, not with clothing, more of the genes.

00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:51.240
But OK.

00:42:51.240 --> 00:42:57.080
So when we also look and think about the stable
unique identifiers, this is a really big deal.

00:42:57.080 --> 00:43:02.660
There's actually a big conference coming up
in Iceland next week.

00:43:02.660 --> 00:43:07.490
But here is a set of numbers that you may
or may not recognize.

00:43:07.490 --> 00:43:08.920
There is a telephone number.

00:43:08.920 --> 00:43:13.980
And in my telephone I see that number, and
it has a little translation.

00:43:13.980 --> 00:43:20.490
I used to be able to remember these numbers,
but I don't anymore because now my phone says,

00:43:20.490 --> 00:43:23.300
oh, it's your mom calling.

00:43:23.300 --> 00:43:24.670
Great.

00:43:24.670 --> 00:43:34.900
So that number has been translated by my phone
to a label, Mom, or Jane Doe, or whoever.

00:43:34.900 --> 00:43:39.380
We don't know this number, but we should because
we go to it every day.

00:43:39.380 --> 00:43:45.430
This is the NCBI server at the National Library
of Medicine, which houses PubMed.

00:43:45.430 --> 00:43:48.040
So we're on this particular number a whole
lot.

00:43:48.040 --> 00:43:55.780
But when we type in Google, when we Google
the word PubMed, this is actually the number

00:43:55.780 --> 00:43:56.780
that we're hitting.

00:43:56.780 --> 00:44:01.030
So each database server has a number, it's
an IP number.

00:44:01.030 --> 00:44:06.450
And we no longer understand that that number
exists, but believe me, it does.

00:44:06.450 --> 00:44:14.859
And Phil Borne here, in this particular perspective,
has told us that essentially each of the items

00:44:14.859 --> 00:44:17.890
needs this kind of number.

00:44:17.890 --> 00:44:22.770
Data software narratives, all sorts of things,
must be uniquely identified.

00:44:22.770 --> 00:44:24.170
They must be sharable.

00:44:24.170 --> 00:44:27.550
Because this is how computers talk to one
another.

00:44:27.550 --> 00:44:35.510
So identifiers-- all the paper identifiers
of us, identifiers of items within the paper--

00:44:35.510 --> 00:44:42.750
really can tell you a whole lot that pretext
or beautiful prose may not be able to tell

00:44:42.750 --> 00:44:44.770
you if you're a computer.

00:44:44.770 --> 00:44:48.040
So we have some nice examples here.

00:44:48.040 --> 00:44:55.040
The re15869 is a snip inside of the BRCA2
gene.

00:44:55.040 --> 00:45:00.400
The NCBI tax on 9606 corresponds to human.

00:45:00.400 --> 00:45:05.920
So if these two identifiers are actually inside
of a particular paper, a machine can tell

00:45:05.920 --> 00:45:10.710
you that this paper reference is a cancer
causing snip in humans.

00:45:10.710 --> 00:45:14.390
Now if you're reading one paper, not a big
deal.

00:45:14.390 --> 00:45:16.170
You can probably read that.

00:45:16.170 --> 00:45:21.090
But it turns out that about a million papers
are published every year in PubMed.

00:45:21.090 --> 00:45:23.869
More than a million now, actually.

00:45:23.869 --> 00:45:27.450
Which ones do you really want to read?

00:45:27.450 --> 00:45:33.540
Is there some way that we can know better
about which ones we want to read?

00:45:33.540 --> 00:45:38.290
Maybe there are some colleagues that you didn't
know you had from another part of the world.

00:45:38.290 --> 00:45:43.359
We actually might need to get some assistance
from Siri-like robots.

00:45:43.359 --> 00:45:49.080
Siri helps me navigate my way through
different parts of different cities, tells

00:45:49.080 --> 00:45:53.410
me which restaurants are reasonable or within
a particular price range.

00:45:53.410 --> 00:45:59.090
Why can't Siri help me find good papers on
PubMed?

00:45:59.090 --> 00:46:08.150
So stable, unique identifiers are way that
we can actually start to understand publications

00:46:08.150 --> 00:46:12.590
free text without doing a whole lot of data
wrangling.

00:46:12.590 --> 00:46:17.130
Usually curators and data wranglers turn text
and data into linked data objects by adding

00:46:17.130 --> 00:46:20.530
these kind of identifiers, but anyone can
do this.

00:46:20.530 --> 00:46:24.960
So for example, ORCID is a researcher ID.

00:46:24.960 --> 00:46:30.150
If you do not have your ORCID, I would highly
recommend that you type ORCID into Google

00:46:30.150 --> 00:46:31.920
and you try to find that.

00:46:31.920 --> 00:46:40.270
Because ORCIDs are a way to disambiguate the
John Smiths, the Wangs of the world, and even

00:46:40.270 --> 00:46:41.270
people like myself.

00:46:41.270 --> 00:46:51.790
I certainly have an ORCID, and I put all my
papers into my ORCID record so that I understand

00:46:51.790 --> 00:46:54.650
what is my intellectual output.

00:46:54.650 --> 00:46:59.410
And I would encourage again everyone to do
the same.

00:46:59.410 --> 00:47:05.411
Grant numbers are another type of number,
and I know that NIH has been asking for a

00:47:05.411 --> 00:47:10.670
very long time about making sure that we get
the grant numbers, and we put those in properly,

00:47:10.670 --> 00:47:16.680
and they go into PubMed, because then they
can ask very straightforward questions.

00:47:16.680 --> 00:47:21.310
What has been produced out of this particular
funding mechanism?

00:47:21.310 --> 00:47:24.260
What is the paper, what is the data that has
been produced?

00:47:24.260 --> 00:47:27.470
So these are things to tag a particular paper.

00:47:27.470 --> 00:47:35.590
DOI is a way, again, to tag a particular paper,
just like PubMed ID, those are both nice identifiers.

00:47:35.590 --> 00:47:38.380
GeneIDs will tag things inside of papers.

00:47:38.380 --> 00:47:41.720
SNIPIDs will tag things inside of papers.

00:47:41.720 --> 00:47:48.170
And those kind of IDs actually disambiguate
what it is that the author is saying.

00:47:48.170 --> 00:47:54.740
We have another type of ID called the RRID,
which tags the research resources, the antibodies,

00:47:54.740 --> 00:47:58.820
cell lines, organism stocks, of a particular
paper.

00:47:58.820 --> 00:48:02.230
Again, so that you can ask fairly simple questions.

00:48:02.230 --> 00:48:05.210
Which gene were you really talking about?

00:48:05.210 --> 00:48:06.470
Which snip is that?

00:48:06.470 --> 00:48:10.450
Is it this particular codon, or is this codon?

00:48:10.450 --> 00:48:15.470
We don't know that unless we actually put
in a snip ID.

00:48:15.470 --> 00:48:20.450
For RRIDs, antibodies, we all know that antibodies
are really hard defined inside of papers.

00:48:20.450 --> 00:48:27.040
But an identifier, RRID, when you look that
up, add it to your paper, it starts to become

00:48:27.040 --> 00:48:28.540
like open data.

00:48:28.540 --> 00:48:34.500
That paper starts to essentially add to the
total amount of knowledge about that particular

00:48:34.500 --> 00:48:37.280
reagent, about that particular cell line.

00:48:37.280 --> 00:48:42.740
And we know that this is one of the big pushes
to disambiguate some of the things that are

00:48:42.740 --> 00:48:46.250
inside of papers and to make them more understandable.

00:48:46.250 --> 00:48:51.920
So now we can talk a little bit about the
closed world versus open world.

00:48:51.920 --> 00:48:56.830
These are assumptions that are being made
by databases.

00:48:56.830 --> 00:49:02.790
A database assumes-- especially a relational
database assumes-- that the answer to your

00:49:02.790 --> 00:49:05.320
question is inside of the database.

00:49:05.320 --> 00:49:09.349
This is what's called the closed world assumption.

00:49:09.349 --> 00:49:11.680
How much money do I have in the bank?

00:49:11.680 --> 00:49:15.330
You have x amount of money in the bank.

00:49:15.330 --> 00:49:18.251
That is the kind of question that a database
is really, really

00:49:18.251 --> 00:49:20.710
good at answering.

00:49:20.710 --> 00:49:27.000
Now if I want to know all genes related to
Parkinson's disease, that's a little bit of

00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:28.470
a different question.

00:49:28.470 --> 00:49:31.080
That's a biological question.

00:49:31.080 --> 00:49:37.790
And it assumes that the answer would be most
likely not in a database.

00:49:37.790 --> 00:49:40.220
Certainly not in one database.

00:49:40.220 --> 00:49:44.940
Maybe not even in the totality of all of the
databases out there.

00:49:44.940 --> 00:49:51.420
So the literature actually covers a small
part of all biological knowledge.

00:49:51.420 --> 00:49:57.150
And traditional databases still, even with
the multitude of these databases, only cover

00:49:57.150 --> 00:50:02.190
a small part of what's known in literature.

00:50:02.190 --> 00:50:11.750
So when we ask databases big open world questions
which really are biological questions, we

00:50:11.750 --> 00:50:17.480
might be running into some assumption problems.

00:50:17.480 --> 00:50:25.290
Because we want to know as biologists the
open world things, but databases are very,

00:50:25.290 --> 00:50:31.670
very good at telling us about the closed world,
about the world that's inside of the database.

00:50:31.670 --> 00:50:37.240
So exploration and visualization.

00:50:37.240 --> 00:50:44.940
It's not actually completely useful.

00:50:44.940 --> 00:50:52.670
But let's see how close we can get to an open
world with some of the bigger databases with

00:50:52.670 --> 00:50:55.050
some of the warehouses.

00:50:55.050 --> 00:50:59.470
So when we start to look at data analysis,
and we start to explore it, what are the sorts

00:50:59.470 --> 00:51:05.700
of things that you can do when we have a lot
of data together?

00:51:05.700 --> 00:51:13.080
And this is one of the gap analyses that we
did with the Neuroscience Information Framework

00:51:13.080 --> 00:51:14.080
data.

00:51:14.080 --> 00:51:20.260
So each of these columns actually represents
a single database.

00:51:20.260 --> 00:51:23.390
And some of them obviously have more data,
some of them have less data.

00:51:23.390 --> 00:51:27.730
So that the darker the green, the more the
data.

00:51:27.730 --> 00:51:33.240
And then here in this particular column, there's
a whole slew of labels.

00:51:33.240 --> 00:51:37.330
And now each of these labels is related to
the brain.

00:51:37.330 --> 00:51:44.260
And in fact, some of them are actually linked
to forebrain, some of them are midbrain, and

00:51:44.260 --> 00:51:46.770
some of them are hindbrain structures.

00:51:46.770 --> 00:51:47.900
So we understand that.

00:51:47.900 --> 00:51:51.990
And there's the magnitude of the data.

00:51:51.990 --> 00:51:58.180
So when we look across all data sources--
so this is around 100 and some odd different

00:51:58.180 --> 00:52:00.210
data sources.

00:52:00.210 --> 00:52:08.310
And we say, well, what do we know about any
structure in the forebrain, versus any structure

00:52:08.310 --> 00:52:12.760
in the midbrain, and any structure in the
hindbrain?

00:52:12.760 --> 00:52:16.359
Do we know more about forebrain or midbrain?

00:52:16.359 --> 00:52:24.640
It turns out that if you then count up the
total number of data points that are essentially

00:52:24.640 --> 00:52:29.490
known about the forebrain, it turns out to
be much, much larger than the data points

00:52:29.490 --> 00:52:32.450
known about the midbrain.

00:52:32.450 --> 00:52:34.590
And here's the thing, though.

00:52:34.590 --> 00:52:42.220
When we look at connectivity matrices in the
brain, it turns out that the midbrain is actually

00:52:42.220 --> 00:52:44.660
really highly connected.

00:52:44.660 --> 00:52:47.550
So we should know more about it.

00:52:47.550 --> 00:52:51.550
But when we look at the data, we don't know
as much about it as we do about forebrain

00:52:51.550 --> 00:52:52.950
structures.

00:52:52.950 --> 00:53:04.840
So because we have an ontology where we know
what we should know, and because we have a

00:53:04.840 --> 00:53:12.490
lot of different databases all in one system,
we can actually know what is labeled, what

00:53:12.490 --> 00:53:19.770
is a part of the data structure in each of
those particular databases.

00:53:19.770 --> 00:53:26.950
Then we can actually start to do a gap analysis,
which is, where should we look?

00:53:26.950 --> 00:53:33.010
Perhaps the midbrain would be a great place
to look, because there isn't as much data

00:53:33.010 --> 00:53:39.440
covering midbrain
structures, and they're likely very important.

00:53:39.440 --> 00:53:43.170
So we can do a lot of other fun things.

00:53:43.170 --> 00:53:48.960
There is a concept of a homunculus in neuroscience,
and a homunculus is the representation of

00:53:48.960 --> 00:53:50.640
the body on the brain.

00:53:50.640 --> 00:53:56.599
So each animal has a representation of the
skin of the body, and we know that when we

00:53:56.599 --> 00:54:02.070
actually redraw this representation of the
body, it's not exactly proportional to the

00:54:02.070 --> 00:54:03.510
skin that actually covers it.

00:54:03.510 --> 00:54:09.110
So we know that the lips, the tongue, the
hands, the fingers are very, very well represented

00:54:09.110 --> 00:54:10.110
in the brain.

00:54:10.110 --> 00:54:13.930
So they're drawn in a homunculus as very large.

00:54:13.930 --> 00:54:21.320
Whereas you know the back and the stomach
are actually very small compared to the amount

00:54:21.320 --> 00:54:29.060
of brain that actually covers understanding
and processing of the information from, say,

00:54:29.060 --> 00:54:30.230
the lips.

00:54:30.230 --> 00:54:35.810
So there is a homunculus in data.

00:54:35.810 --> 00:54:42.730
So each of these points-- if we look at each
of these points on this particular graph--

00:54:42.730 --> 00:54:46.630
is a disease term like Alzheimer's.

00:54:46.630 --> 00:54:50.369
And there's lots and lots of disease terms,
because there is an ontology, so it's a very

00:54:50.369 --> 00:54:53.520
long list of all sorts of disease terms.

00:54:53.520 --> 00:55:02.140
And so what we've asked is let's look at all
of the terms across all of the databases.

00:55:02.140 --> 00:55:05.450
And let's plot that versus Reporter.

00:55:05.450 --> 00:55:11.460
Now, Reporter gives us an idea of what grants
have been funded, and they also cover disease

00:55:11.460 --> 00:55:12.880
terms.

00:55:12.880 --> 00:55:17.990
So it turns out, now I haven't done a full
correlation on this, but it looks pretty darn

00:55:17.990 --> 00:55:22.640
good that if you fund it, data will come.

00:55:22.640 --> 00:55:31.910
So it turns out that if there are grants that
are actually funded to look at a particular

00:55:31.910 --> 00:55:34.930
disease, you tend to get more data points.

00:55:34.930 --> 00:55:39.340
So the data points are here and the number
of grants funded are here.

00:55:39.340 --> 00:55:46.410
So that kind of tells us that what we fund
is actually very, very important in terms

00:55:46.410 --> 00:55:50.660
of what we see in the data space.

00:55:50.660 --> 00:55:54.530
And I want to hit this one more time.

00:55:54.530 --> 00:56:00.330
Comprehensive indices are usually the most
critical to biologists.

00:56:00.330 --> 00:56:05.060
So one such comprehensive index is the antibody
registry.

00:56:05.060 --> 00:56:11.530
This has about 2.5 million antibodies, which
is about as many antibodies as we can probably

00:56:11.530 --> 00:56:15.080
get to.

00:56:15.080 --> 00:56:19.720
There are certainly some things missing, but
it's something that's being worked on.

00:56:19.720 --> 00:56:24.300
But you can ask certain questions, such as,
well, let's see what all the different genes

00:56:24.300 --> 00:56:30.900
are, and this is running the 30,000 human
genes, this is down sampled, across any results

00:56:30.900 --> 00:56:33.240
from the antibody registry.

00:56:33.240 --> 00:56:37.470
So how many antibodies are there for most
genes?

00:56:37.470 --> 00:56:42.660
Turns out for about half of the genes, we
actually have between 11 and 100 antibodies.

00:56:42.660 --> 00:56:48.730
So there's pretty good coverage of this type
of reagent for anything you might want to

00:56:48.730 --> 00:56:49.730
hit.

00:56:49.730 --> 00:56:54.869
Some of the pseudogenes, and the predictive
genes have not been covered.

00:56:54.869 --> 00:57:01.380
But most of the genome is very well covered
by lots and lots of reagents.

00:57:01.380 --> 00:57:04.190
So fun things that we can know.

00:57:04.190 --> 00:57:08.609
So databases really provide structured data,
and therefore they can tell us what data is

00:57:08.609 --> 00:57:09.849
available.

00:57:09.849 --> 00:57:14.510
Warehouses start to actually show places where
there is no data.

00:57:14.510 --> 00:57:20.150
But what if we don't have a database to cover
the thing that want to know about?

00:57:20.150 --> 00:57:22.369
What if there is only text?

00:57:22.369 --> 00:57:25.390
Well, this is where we would get into natural
language processing

00:57:25.390 --> 00:57:30.040
and a talk in about five weeks.

00:57:30.040 --> 00:57:33.560
So natural language processing is a whole
field of computer science.

00:57:33.560 --> 00:57:37.430
Deals with artificial intelligence, computational
linguistics.

00:57:37.430 --> 00:57:44.119
It's very, very concerned with interactions
between computers and human languages.

00:57:44.119 --> 00:57:49.600
So remember your second grade teacher, your
third grade teacher made you do these diagrams

00:57:49.600 --> 00:57:50.980
of sentences.

00:57:50.980 --> 00:57:56.480
Well, it turns out that computer scientists
have been able to train computers to do this.

00:57:56.480 --> 00:58:02.359
So had I known that I could have cheated in
second grade, maybe I would have done it.

00:58:02.359 --> 00:58:07.440
But now there are lots and lots of different
methods.

00:58:07.440 --> 00:58:15.980
The Stanford parser and others are available
to researchers that would like to figure out

00:58:15.980 --> 00:58:18.700
how to diagram sentences.

00:58:18.700 --> 00:58:25.089
There are also a lot of statistical algorithms,
like LDA and others, that actually show which

00:58:25.089 --> 00:58:30.930
part of a particular passage, which part of
a paragraph is more important than the other

00:58:30.930 --> 00:58:31.930
parts.

00:58:31.930 --> 00:58:34.040
So they pull out key words.

00:58:34.040 --> 00:58:41.330
And so what I wanted to show you is just a
couple of examples on how this is being used

00:58:41.330 --> 00:58:45.620
today inside of biological databases.

00:58:45.620 --> 00:58:56.410
So one of the issues with functional magnetic
resonance imaging studies is that everyone

00:58:56.410 --> 00:59:04.880
tries to tell us which parts of the brain
lit up, which parts use up more glucose, when

00:59:04.880 --> 00:59:07.460
a particular task is being done.

00:59:07.460 --> 00:59:13.960
So now there's a lot of people who do this
kind of work, and they all try to tag these

00:59:13.960 --> 00:59:16.280
different parts in the brain.

00:59:16.280 --> 00:59:22.620
But getting that information into a database
is actually relatively difficult and really

00:59:22.620 --> 00:59:24.119
time consuming.

00:59:24.119 --> 00:59:32.050
So Neurosynth, Talia Coney's database, has
actually utilized a lot of text mining in

00:59:32.050 --> 00:59:38.470
order to make this process much faster and
simpler and create a complete dataset.

00:59:38.470 --> 00:59:44.330
Not maybe as accurate as some of the human
curated, but certainly it's complete because

00:59:44.330 --> 00:59:53.530
it houses all of the papers, and it basically
gets papers into the database very, very quickly.

00:59:53.530 --> 01:00:03.030
So what it does is it removes a lot of the
talairach coordinates for the particular places

01:00:03.030 --> 01:00:09.630
in the brain where the activation is actually
being reported.

01:00:09.630 --> 01:00:14.310
Then it does a bunch of text mining and statistical
analysis to figure out which are the most

01:00:14.310 --> 01:00:21.220
important terms in that particular text that
are being discussed.

01:00:21.220 --> 01:00:24.050
So here is an example for working memory.

01:00:24.050 --> 01:00:31.370
Now, working memory has about 901 studies
that have been reported on it.

01:00:31.370 --> 01:00:39.980
And Tal's work essentially shows that this
particular term is most relevant to the particular

01:00:39.980 --> 01:00:47.190
fMRI images, and then he replots this onto
a human brain, which is great.

01:00:47.190 --> 01:00:53.170
So here are the activation foci for these
901 studies, and they actually do cluster.

01:00:53.170 --> 01:00:57.480
So even though the data is coming from all
kinds of papers, and even though this is all

01:00:57.480 --> 01:01:03.839
computer generated data, the computer is actually
pulling all of these 901 papers.

01:01:03.839 --> 01:01:07.370
He didn't have to do this manually.

01:01:07.370 --> 01:01:11.550
But what happens is, we can actually see a
pretty good clustering of what are the sorts

01:01:11.550 --> 01:01:16.270
of things that are being reported about working
memory on the brain.

01:01:16.270 --> 01:01:19.460
What are some of the other applications?

01:01:19.460 --> 01:01:25.260
Well, there is a program called Textpresso,
and Textpresso's very cool.

01:01:25.260 --> 01:01:29.290
There are lots of other tools that can actually
do this, there some that are commercial.

01:01:29.290 --> 01:01:33.240
But this gives you an idea
of what are some of the other things that

01:01:33.240 --> 01:01:36.170
text mining can do?

01:01:36.170 --> 01:01:42.160
So when we have text, we can diagram sentences
we can figure out what's a noun we can figure

01:01:42.160 --> 01:01:43.180
out what's a verb, et cetera.

01:01:43.180 --> 01:01:51.130
But what we can also do is we can figure out
what are the terms inside of that particular

01:01:51.130 --> 01:01:55.280
paper that are relevant to me?

01:01:55.280 --> 01:02:02.570
And I can ask questions such as, give me all
sentences, Textpresso, that you know about

01:02:02.570 --> 01:02:06.369
which list Parkinson's and a brain area.

01:02:06.369 --> 01:02:09.839
Now, I'm asking for any brain area.

01:02:09.839 --> 01:02:12.119
I'm not asking for the word, brain area.

01:02:12.119 --> 01:02:16.650
If you typed this into Google, you will get
brain area Parkinson's.

01:02:16.650 --> 01:02:24.050
But what you're really asking is, I want Parkinson's
disease, and tell me which brain areas are

01:02:24.050 --> 01:02:26.230
most relevant to it.

01:02:26.230 --> 01:02:32.849
So here's a sentence, one of the many hits
that Textpresso gave, and here is a match

01:02:32.849 --> 01:02:38.510
for a sentence, Parkinson's, and talks about
the substantia nigra pars compacta.

01:02:38.510 --> 01:02:43.710
Now, how do we know that the substantia nigra
pars compacta is a brain area?

01:02:43.710 --> 01:02:47.670
Well, remember back I showed you that ontology?

01:02:47.670 --> 01:02:53.640
Well the ontology says, substantia nigra pars
compacta is part of the brain.

01:02:53.640 --> 01:02:54.890
So it is a brain area.

01:02:54.890 --> 01:03:02.550
So what we can do is we can have large lists
of terms that are related to a particular

01:03:02.550 --> 01:03:12.030
thing we want to know, like brain area, gene,
expression type, et cetera.

01:03:12.030 --> 01:03:18.810
So there are large lookup lists which we can
actually feed into a text analysis system

01:03:18.810 --> 01:03:24.240
to light up the kinds of things that we might
want to know about particular sentences.

01:03:24.240 --> 01:03:30.880
And again, we're producing papers at a million
papers per year.

01:03:30.880 --> 01:03:35.359
Something needs to help us sort through a
lot of this data.

01:03:35.359 --> 01:03:42.060
And systems such as text mining systems are
actually starting to get pretty good, and

01:03:42.060 --> 01:03:47.240
are able to show us some of the things that
we might not otherwise know.

01:03:47.240 --> 01:03:52.820
So with that, what I wanted to do is I wanted
to thank you very much for listening, for

01:03:52.820 --> 01:03:53.820
tuning in.

01:03:53.820 --> 01:04:00.480
Thank you for the opportunity to talk to you
all, and I hope you tune in for the rest of

01:04:00.480 --> 01:04:01.690
this series.

01:04:01.690 --> 01:04:05.330
Thank you so much, Dr. Bandrowski.

01:04:05.330 --> 01:04:09.359
We are out of time now, so we won't be able
to take questions.

01:04:09.359 --> 01:04:15.420
But I think that's OK, because you were so
incredibly clear on this, and we all really

01:04:15.420 --> 01:04:17.950
appreciate this talk.

01:04:17.950 --> 01:04:21.650
So thank you very much.

01:04:21.650 --> 01:04:27.020
And to everyone out there listening, thanks
for tuning in, and we will see you next week.

01:04:27.020 --> 01:04:27.890
Bye bye.

